window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.6","title":"Expected Value and the Law of Large Numbers","goal":"Compute expected values for a die and for roulette, then watch the running average of simulated die rolls settle on 3.5 (the Law of Large Numbers).","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Expected value of a die","text":"E(X) is each outcome times its probability, added up. A die never shows 3.5, but 3.5 is where the average of many rolls ends up.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nx = np.arange(1, 7)\np = np.full(6, 1 / 6)\n\nprint('x * P(x):', (x * p).round(4))\nprint('E(X) =', round((x * p).sum(), 4))","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Is a bet worth taking?","text":"A $1 roulette bet on one number wins $35 with probability 1/38 and loses $1 otherwise. A negative expected value means a long-run loss for the player: the house edge.","code":"win, lose = 35, -1\np_win = 1 / 38\n\nev = win * p_win + lose * (1 - p_win)\nprint(f'expected gain per $1 bet = {ev:.4f}')\nprint(f'that is a loss of about {abs(ev) * 100:.1f} cents per dollar')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate and track the running average","text":"We roll a die 5,000 times with a seeded generator. The running average after n rolls is the cumulative total divided by n. Early values jump around, later ones stay near 3.5.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nrolls = rng.integers(1, 7, size=5000)\nrunning = np.cumsum(rolls) / np.arange(1, 5001)\n\nfor n in [5, 50, 500, 5000]:\n    print(f'after {n:>4} rolls: average = {running[n - 1]:.3f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the running average","text":"The line wobbles wildly at first and then flattens onto the expected value. Individual rolls stay random; the average becomes predictable.","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(np.arange(1, 5001), running, color='#2563eb', lw=1.2)\nplt.axhline(3.5, color='#dc2626', ls='--', label='E(X) = 3.5')\nplt.xscale('log')\nplt.xlabel('number of rolls (log scale)')\nplt.ylabel('running average')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"x <- 1:6\nsum(x * 1/6)                  # E(X) of a die\n35 * (1/38) + (-1) * (37/38)  # roulette\n\nset.seed(42)\nrolls <- sample(6, 5000, replace = TRUE)\ncumsum(rolls)[c(5, 50, 500, 5000)] / c(5, 50, 500, 5000)","expect":"3.5; -0.0526; running averages close to 3.5 by 5000 rolls (not identical to Python's)","note":"The two expected values are exact and match Python. R draws different random rolls, so its running averages are close to 3.5 but not the same as Python's."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (expected values); Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives (the simulated mean)","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #i = 1 TO 5000.\nCOMPUTE roll = TRUNC(RV.UNIFORM(1, 7)).\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=roll.\nCOMPUTE ev_die = (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6.\nCOMPUTE roulette = 35/38 - 37/38.\nFORMATS ev_die roulette (F8.4).\nLIST VARIABLES=ev_die roulette /CASES=FROM 1 TO 1.","note":"Exact: 3.5 and -0.0526. The simulated mean of roll is close to 3.5 but not identical to Python's, and SPSS has no menu for a running-average plot."},"stata":{"code":"display (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6\ndisplay 35/38 - 37/38\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 5000\ngen roll = ceil(6*runiform())\ngen running = sum(roll) / _n\nlist running in 5\nlist running in 5000\nsummarize roll","note":"The two display lines are exact (3.5 and -.0526316). In Stata sum() is a running total, so dividing by _n gives the running average. The simulated values are close to 3.5 but differ from Python's."},"try":["Change the random seed from 42 to 1. Does the early part of the curve change? Does the end?","Replace the die with a roulette bet (+35 with probability 1/38, otherwise -1) and predict where the running average heads."]});
